<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potsdam Declaration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> Declaration was the statement issued on 26 July 1945 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> setting out the terms for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It demanded the unconditional surrender of the armed forces, disarmament, occupation and the removal of militarist leaders, threatening 'prompt and utter destruction' otherwise. Japan accepted it on 14 August 1945, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-declaration-of-war-on-japan">Soviet declaration of war</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration.jpg" alt="Potsdam Declaration" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Harry_S._Truman_and_Prime_Minister_Winston_Churchill_on_the_steps_of_the_%22Little_White_House,%22_the..._-_NARA_-_198764.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At the start of the Potsdam Conference the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> delegation considered a proclamation demanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s unconditional surrender in the name of the United States, the United Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The text went through many drafts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> declined to endorse it, since the Soviet Union was not yet at war with Japan, and committed instead to subscribing once it entered the war. On 24 July President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> a draft, and the American delegation adopted all the British amendments.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> was not at the conference, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was asked by telegram. He agreed to one amendment, listing the President of the National Government of the Republic of China before the British prime minister. Churchill authorised the text on 26 July before submitting his resignation that evening, after his unexpected election defeat. The signatures of Chiang and Churchill were in fact in Truman's handwriting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The declaration demanded the elimination for all time of the authority of those who had misled the Japanese people into world conquest, the occupation of points in Japanese territory to be designated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and the limiting of Japanese sovereignty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> and minor islands. Japanese forces were to be disarmed and sent home. It stated that the Allies did not intend the Japanese to be enslaved as a race or destroyed as a nation, but that war criminals would receive stern justice.</p><p>It also offered the revival of democratic tendencies, freedom of speech, religion and thought, industries that would sustain the economy but not rearmament, and the withdrawal of occupying forces once a peaceful and responsible government had been established. It never mentioned the emperor, and left unstated the number of occupation points, the fate of the minor islands and the form of Allied control. The warning of prompt and utter destruction has been read as a veiled reference to the atomic bomb, which was not named.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The declaration was broadcast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and more than 3 million leaflets describing it were dropped, but it was never sent through diplomatic channels. In Tokyo, Foreign Minister Shigenori Tōgō and others felt it must be accepted, but the war minister and the chiefs of staff opposed it. Prime Minister Kantarō Suzuki described the policy as mokusatsu, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took as rejection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> was bombed on 6 August and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> on 9 August, the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> declared war on Japan.</p><p>The emperor announced acceptance in a radio broadcast, and surrender documents were signed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uss-missouri">USS Missouri</a> on 2 September 1945. The declaration then served as the legal basis for the occupation's reforms. Historians still debate whether the demand for unconditional surrender stalled possible peace negotiations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Potsdam Declaration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan's government notified the Allies that it accepted the <b>Potsdam Declaration</b>, and President Truman announced the news that evening.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-14">14 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They settled the occupation of Germany and issued the <b>Potsdam Declaration</b> demanding Japan's unconditional surrender.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan accepted the <b>Potsdam Declaration</b> on 15 August 1945 and signed the instrument of surrender on 2 September aboard the USS Missouri.</blockquote><a href="/state/occupied-japan">Occupied Japan</a></li></ul></section>
